### Declare a BullMQ consumer class with @Processor

Source: https://github.com/nestjs/docs.nestjs.com/blob/master/content/application/queues.md

Decorate a class with @Processor to define a queue consumer for a specific queue. Consumers must be registered as NestJS providers so @nestjs/bullmq can pick them up.

```typescript
import { Processor } from '@nestjs/bullmq';

@Processor('audio')
export class AudioConsumer {}
```

--------------------------------

### Subscribe to worker events using @OnWorkerEvent

Source: https://github.com/nestjs/docs.nestjs.com/blob/master/content/application/queues.md

Worker events must be declared inside a consumer class decorated with @Processor(). Use @OnWorkerEvent() to handle events such as a job entering the active state in a queue.

```typescript
import { Processor, WorkerHost, OnWorkerEvent } from '@nestjs/bullmq';
import { Job } from 'bullmq';

@Processor('audio')
export class AudioConsumer extends WorkerHost {
  @OnWorkerEvent('active')
  onActive(job: Job) {
    console.log(
      `Processing job ${job.id} of type ${job.name} with data ${job.data}...`,
    );
  }

  // ...
}
```

--------------------------------

### Register a queue with BullModule.registerQueue()

Source: https://github.com/nestjs/docs.nestjs.com/blob/master/content/application/queues.md

Registers a named queue with BullModule to instantiate the queue and provide an injection token for producers and consumers.

```typescript
BullModule.registerQueue({
  name: 'audio',
});
```

--------------------------------

### Create standalone AWS Lambda handler with NestFactory.createApplicationContext

Source: https://github.com/nestjs/docs.nestjs.com/blob/master/content/faq/serverless.md

Use NestFactory.createApplicationContext() to run lightweight Lambda functions without starting an entire HTTP server. Note that createApplicationContext() does not wrap controller methods with enhancers such as guards, interceptors, or pipes.

```typescript
@@filename(main)
import { HttpStatus } from '@nestjs/common';
import { NestFactory } from '@nestjs/core';
import type { Callback, Context, Handler } from 'aws-lambda';
import { AppModule } from './app.module.js';
import { AppService } from './app.service.js';

export const handler: Handler = async (
  event: any,
  context: Context,
  callback: Callback,
) => {
  const appContext = await NestFactory.createApplicationContext(AppModule);
  const appService = appContext.get(AppService);

  return {
    body: appService.getHello(),
    statusCode: HttpStatus.OK,
  };
};
```

--------------------------------

### Close Standalone Application Context using app.close()

Source: https://github.com/nestjs/docs.nestjs.com/blob/master/content/application-context.md

Triggers NestJS shutdown lifecycle hooks and allows the Node.js process to exit cleanly after execution completes. Call app.close() at the end of the bootstrap function for scripts and CRON tasks.

```typescript
@@filename()
async function bootstrap() {
  const app = await NestFactory.createApplicationContext(AppModule);
  // application logic...
  await app.close();
}
await bootstrap();
```
